//
// @PLN89 differential-oracle corpus — AXIS: a program that HALTS.
//
// @ORACLE_HALTS: the halt IS the subject — a failed `assert` must render and exit
// identically on both backends.
//
// The oracle has compared exit codes since it was built, and until this file no program
// in the corpus ever exited non-zero: all thirty ran to completion, so the exit-code
// channel agreed by never being exercised.  That is the same shape as a green check that
// examined nothing, and it hid a real divergence — a failed `assert` exited 1 on the
// interpreter and 101 on `--native`, on every program, for as long as both existed.
//
// Output is printed BEFORE the halt so the stdout channel is checked too: a backend that
// halted too early, or buffered its output away on the abort path, differs here as well
// as on the code.  Both halves matter — an exit code alone cannot tell a program that
// stopped at the right place from one that never started.

fn main() {
  print("before=1\n");
  n = 6;
  print("n={n}\n");
  assert(n == 7, "halt on purpose: n was {n}");
  print("unreachable\n");
}
